<article>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="txt">The problem</span></h2>
    <p class="lede">Every session with a coding agent starts from zero. The model reasons well in the moment and knows nothing about last week — not because it can't remember, but because by default there's nowhere for a memory to <em>live</em> between conversations.</p>
    <p>That's fine for a one-off task. It stops being fine the moment the agent becomes a daily tool spanning a dozen threads of work at once, because "start from zero" and "there's a decision from three weeks ago that still matters" collide constantly. The obvious workaround — paste more context into every prompt — doesn't scale, and it puts the burden on exactly the wrong side: a human re-deriving state a machine should be tracking.</p>
    <p>What follows is the other approach: give the agent a real, external, versioned memory, and make it responsible for keeping that memory current. It's a git repository of markdown files, a couple of read/write conventions, and an orchestration pattern for the agent work that touches it. None of the individual pieces are exotic. What's worth writing up is why they're shaped the way they are — mostly lessons from watching the naive version of each one fail.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">01</span><span class="txt">The core bet: a git repo, not a database</span></h2>
    <p>Two decisions sit upstream of everything else. Memory lives in plain markdown files, not database rows or vector embeddings. And the store is a normal git repository, synced the ordinary way — commits, branches, pull requests.</p>
    <p>Neither is a clever choice, and that's on purpose. A markdown file is the one format a human and a language model can both read and edit without a translation layer — no query language, no admin UI, no export step. A git repo already solves versioning, diffing, blame, merge, and multi-device sync: problems a bespoke memory system would otherwise reinvent, worse, from scratch.</p>
    <p>RAG over a vector store is a good answer to "search a large, mostly-static corpus." It's a worse answer to "keep a small, living, editable record current," because it adds an indexing step, a similarity metric, and a re-embedding pipeline in a place where <code class="inline">git log</code> and <code class="inline">grep</code> already do the job.</p>
    <p>The result is unglamorous on purpose.</p>

    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      <p>The store is a directory of markdown files that would still be useful if every model on earth vanished tomorrow.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>That's not a throwaway line — it's the actual test a piece of this system has to pass before it's allowed in. Every format choice, every sync mechanism, every convention below got measured against it: would this still make sense to a human with nothing but a text editor and <code class="inline">git log</code>? If the answer was no, the piece didn't ship, no matter how much cleaner it would have made the agent-facing side.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">02</span><span class="txt">Two tiers of instruction</span></h2>
    <p>Not all context is the same kind of fact, so the system splits it before anything reaches per-topic memory:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A global instruction file</strong>, loaded in every project, answering <em>how should you behave</em> — tone, when to ask before acting, how to handle sensitive data, how to run sub-agents. Behavioral, not situational.</li>
      <li><strong>A project instruction file</strong>, checked into each repo, answering <em>what's true about this codebase</em> — structure, conventions, the session start/end protocol. Anyone working in that repo, human or agent, gets the same brief.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where the two overlap, the project file wins — a repo's own conventions override the general defaults, the same way a linter config in a subdirectory overrides the one at the root. Neither file is allowed to hold facts that belong in the layer below: durable, dated, individual facts.</p>

    <div class="panel">
      <div class="panel-label">excerpt — global instructions (trimmed)</div>
      <pre># Behavioral Rules
- No sycophancy. Say when you're wrong and move on.
- Check a tool is still maintained before recommending it.
- Sensitive data: compute locally, share only aggregates,
  never route secrets through a hosted tool.

# Agent Orchestration
- Spawn sub-agents for parallelizable work; coordinate
  results yourself. Don't do sequentially what can run
  in parallel.
- Match model to task complexity (see §6).</pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">03</span><span class="txt">The part that writes itself</span></h2>
    <p>Instructions describe behavior. A separate mechanism handles <em>facts</em> — decisions, corrections, standing preferences — that accumulate over time and have no business being hardcoded into an instructions file. Each fact is its own markdown file with a small, consistent frontmatter schema:</p>

    <div class="panel">
      <div class="panel-label">auto-memory/tool-currency-check.md</div>
      <pre>---
name: tool-currency-check
description: Verify a tool is still maintained before
  recommending it
type: feedback
tags: [tooling, recommendations, maintenance]
---

Confirmed: recommended a tool that turned out to be in
maintenance-only mode; an actively maintained successor
existed under a different name. One search costs less
than being corrected.</pre>
    </div>

    <p>Recall is deliberately two-tier. An index file holds one line per fact — cheap enough to load in full at the start of every session, so most recall never touches the filesystem beyond one file. When the index doesn't surface a match, the fallback is a grep across frontmatter tags before concluding nothing exists. Fast path first, exhaustive path second — the same shape as an index versus a table scan, run over a folder of markdown instead of a database.</p>
    <p>The harder discipline is what's kept <em>out</em>. Anything the repo or its git history already records is explicitly off-limits for a memory file — code structure, past fixes, commit history. Memory exists for what isn't derivable: a decision's reasoning, a stated preference, a correction. A memory store that also mirrors the codebase is just a second, staler copy of it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">04</span><span class="txt">Derive state, don't hand-maintain it</span></h2>
    <p>The system generates a session-start brief — active projects, what changed last time, anything urgent — from a script, not a hand-kept status doc. The script pulls two things: frontmatter fields (status, priorities, deadlines) and <strong>git commit history</strong> for freshness, rather than a manually typed "last updated" field.</p>
    <p>That second part matters more than it sounds. A hand-maintained timestamp drifts the moment someone forgets to touch it — and someone always eventually forgets. Git history doesn't drift; it's a true, mechanical record of when a file actually changed, which turns "how recently was this touched" from a maintenance chore into a query. The same logic runs one level up: the brief itself is regenerated each session rather than kept as a standing document, so it can never go stale in a way that outlives its own usefulness.</p>
    <p>The general shape: anywhere the system needs to answer "is this current," prefer deriving the answer from a system of record that's mechanically hard to lie to (git, filesystem timestamps, test results) over a field a human or an agent has to remember to update by hand.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">05</span><span class="txt">Multiple surfaces, one sync layer</span></h2>
    <p>The same repo gets written to from more than one place — a primary workstation, an always-on home server, and a phone client for quick edits on the go. All of it is git; there's no separate sync protocol layered on top. What differs is the write path, and it's matched to how much that surface should be trusted unsupervised.</p>
    <p>The workstation and home server commit directly — a human is present, or the change is low-stakes and reviewable after the fact. The phone client, which drives edits through a chat interface with less friction and less scrutiny per edit, opens a <strong>pull request</strong> instead of pushing to the main branch. Same repo, same format, but a review gate appears automatically on the surface where it's needed and stays out of the way on the surfaces where it isn't.</p>

