    <p>The other lesson here came from concurrency, not trust: more than one session can be active against the same repo at once. A branch can change underfoot, a commit from elsewhere can land mid-task, an in-progress stash can vanish because another process touched the same files. The fix isn't a locking mechanism — it's a habit: check actual state (<code class="inline">git status</code>, current branch) immediately before any commit or push rather than trusting what was true at session start, and if something looks off, stop and surface it instead of forcing through. Git already tells you the truth; the discipline is asking it right before you act, not once at the beginning.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">06</span><span class="txt">Orchestrator and sub-agents</span></h2>
    <p>For anything with independent, parallelizable pieces, one agent plans and holds context — the orchestrator — while scoped sub-agents execute pieces and report back. Two rules make this pay for itself rather than just adding ceremony.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-head">Match the model to the task</h3>
    <p>Not every step deserves the most capable model available. A lightweight validation or review pass runs on a fast, cheap model; general implementation and research runs on a mid-tier default; a genuinely hard, ambiguous architecture problem escalates to the frontier model — but only after flagging <em>why</em> it warrants the cost, since that's a spending decision as much as a technical one. Cost-awareness is a first-class routing input, not an afterthought applied after the fact.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-head">Sub-agents return data; only the orchestrator writes</h3>
    <p>This is the load-bearing rule. A sub-agent can read, research, and reason — but it hands its findings back as data, and only the orchestrator is allowed to commit anything to the memory store. It's a security boundary as much as a workflow one: a sub-agent that ingests an untrusted web page or PDF can be manipulated by content embedded in that page, so the boundary between "content I fetched" and "instructions I follow" has to be structural, not a matter of the model staying polite. External content gets wrapped in explicit delimiters before it ever reaches a model, and it's referenced from instructions, never interpolated into them — ordinary prompt-injection hygiene, applied to a system that happens to be personal rather than customer-facing.</p>

