  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="num">07</span><span class="txt">War story: the silent revert</span></h2>
    <p>The most useful bug in this system's life didn't look like a memory-architecture problem at all. A "propose changes" flow — the mechanism that turns an agent's edits into a reviewable pull request — started silently discarding entire sessions of work. Files reverted to their prior state, nothing was committed anywhere, and there was no error. It looked, for a while, like an external process interfering, because the actual pattern was a very convincing coincidence: files that happened to have merged recently "survived," and files that hadn't yet "kept reverting" — a correlation that was real, and exactly backwards as evidence.</p>
    <p>The real cause: a cleanup step that restored the working tree to its pre-change state ran on <em>every</em> exit path out of the function — including the failure path. A pre-flight lint check had been added upstream of the git operations, and it was failing on every single proposal for an environment reason (a spawned linter binary resolving against the wrong runtime version). So the actual sequence, every time, was: make the edits, fail the lint check, revert the files, commit nothing, report nothing. The "survivors" had simply already merged and come back on the next pull, before this bug started; the "victims" hadn't, so the same revert function was the last thing to touch them, over and over.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="mark">the rule</div>
      <p>When something "never works," check whether it's silently switched off, or silently swallowing its own errors, before re-diagnosing the implementation underneath it. A disabled feature and a bare <code class="inline">catch {}</code> both produce symptoms that look exactly like a subtly wrong implementation — and cost far less to rule out first.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The fix had two parts, and only one of them was the actual lint bug. First, the destructive step moved to run only <em>after</em> a local commit exists — so a failing check now leaves the work recoverable on a branch instead of gone. Second, the test suite gained coverage for the failure path specifically: branch created, content correct, nothing pushed to the remote, working tree clean, the user-facing message naming the recovery branch. That path had zero coverage before, which is exactly why a change that silently ate real work could pass a suite of thirty other assertions without tripping any of them.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="txt">What actually carried over</span></h2>
    <p>Stripped of the specifics, the design principles that survived contact with daily use:</p>
    <ul class="principles">
      <li><span class="pn">→</span><span class="pt"><strong>Prefer formats a human can read without a viewer.</strong> Markdown and git over a database, every time the corpus is small enough that this is actually an option.</span></li>
      <li><span class="pn">→</span><span class="pt"><strong>Derive state; don't hand-maintain it.</strong> Anything a script can compute from git history or the filesystem shouldn't also be a field a human has to remember to update.</span></li>
      <li><span class="pn">→</span><span class="pt"><strong>Give every write path a trust level, and match the review gate to it.</strong> The same repo can be commit-direct in one place and PR-gated in another — the gate follows the risk, not a blanket policy.</span></li>
      <li><span class="pn">→</span><span class="pt"><strong>Sub-agents are for parallelism and cost control, not for skipping oversight.</strong> Data flows up; writes flow through exactly one place.</span></li>
      <li><span class="pn">→</span><span class="pt"><strong>Silent failure is the default failure mode to hunt for first.</strong> An "off" switch and a swallowed error both masquerade as a subtler bug than they are.</span></li>
      <li><span class="pn">→</span><span class="pt"><strong>The store doesn't need to be a complete record to be useful.</strong> It only needs to beat starting from zero — and it does, by a wide margin.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 class="section-head"><span class="txt">The honest limits</span></h2>
    <p>This isn't a life-log and doesn't try to be. There's always a gap between what the system knows and what actually happened, because nobody's narrating their day into a markdown file in real time — the store only ever holds what someone deliberately wrote down. That's a feature to design around, not a flaw to apologize for: a system that states its blind spots plainly is more trustworthy than one that quietly implies it sees everything.</p>
    <p>Six months in, the interesting part isn't any single mechanism — it's that the whole thing is boring in the right ways, and still passes the test from the top of this piece: it would still be useful with every model gone. The store doesn't <em>need</em> a dashboard to stay trustworthy — a text editor and <code class="inline">git log</code> are still enough to audit any of it — and that's exactly what makes building a dashboard on top a good idea rather than a crutch: a view layered over a substrate that doesn't depend on it, not a substitute for one that does. That's a separate write-up. What carried this one was that the parts that break are debuggable with tools that predate any of this by decades: <code class="inline">git log</code>, <code class="inline">grep</code>, a test suite.</p>
  </section>

